--- Finding (terminal_control_coloured_text): at the top level, a value that can
--- fail written by key into a map whose values are plain text is refused before
--- the program runs, with words that blame the map's first line:
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike (exit 1):
---     p.co:25:1: cannot reassign 'm' from map[result[str]] to map[str] — variable
---     type cannot change
---       25 │ m = map[k = 'v']
--- Expected (spec, "A write lands at one position": the position may be a map's
--- key, and "a value that can fail is decided where it is written"), in both
--- modes:
---     [k = 'yes']
--- as the list written by position runs (`xs = ['a']` / `xs[0] = maybe[1]` prints
--- ['yes'], and `maybe[0]` there stops with "cannot assign an error value to
--- 'xs' — no"). An empty map whose first write is the failing value (`m = map[]`)
--- runs (a map of values that can fail), as does `m = set[m, 'k', maybe[1]]`.
--- Perhaps the same root as -1.co (the write by key types the map's values as
--- values that can fail); the known open finding about a list of text
--- reassigned a list whose items can fail has the same words.
fn maybe[n]
    if n gt 0
        return 'yes'
    return error['no']

m = map[k = 'v']
m['k'] = maybe[1]
log[m]
